The following are specifically declared to be nuisances:
(1) Toilets, other sanitary facilities, plumbing, or sewers not in compliance with the rules and regulations of the county board of health or the department of human resources;
(2) Conditions conducive to the excessive breeding of rodents, flies, mosquitoes, or other insects and vermin in relation to surrounding conditions as determined by the inspector.
(3) Pollution of the air by gases, vapors, fumes, mists, dust or smoke in quantities sufficient to be disagreeable, discomforting, or detrimental to health or wellbeing in relation to the conditions of the surrounding area, as determined by the inspector.
(4) Trash, garbage, refuse, or any foul, decaying, or putrescent material kept in such quantities or used in such as manner or place as to be or become offensive, objectionable, or detrimental to health or well-being;
(5) Keeping of horses, mules, assess, cows, sheep, goats, hogs, dogs, rabbits, guinea pigs, hamsters, chickens, turkeys, geese, ducks, pigeons, or similar fowl or animals except under the following conditions:
a. Any housing or enclosures used by such animals or fowl shall be well drained, free from accumulations of animal excrement and objectionable odors, and otherwise clean and sanitary. Animal excrement shall be disposed of in a manner approved by the health officer, the building inspector or the enforcement officer;
b. All such animals or fowl, except dogs in areas where confinement of dogs under the rabies control regulations does not apply, shall be kept in adequate enclosures or tethered.
c. Diseased animals or fowl which might infect healthy animals or cause a menace to the public health shall be isolated or destroyed are as the health officer, the building inspector, or the enforcement officer deem necessary.
d. In the case of bona fide licensed pet shops, veterinary hospitals, stockyards, poultry houses, and similar commercial establishments, the health officer may modify these requirements where undue hardship would result from their strict enforcement;
(6) Such other nuisances as are defined in this chapter, by state law, or by the board of commissioners.
(Ord. of 3-6-2018)

719:
(a) The following items are specifically declared to constitute a nuisance:
(1) The making, creating or permitting of any unreasonably loud or unnecessary noise in the county is prohibited.
(2) The making, creating or permitting of any such noise, sound, or vibration of such intensity, duration, frequency, or character that would be harmful and detrimental to the health, welfare, and safety of the inhabitants of the county is prohibited.
(3) It shall be unlawful for any person to willfully make or continue or cause to be made or continued, any loud, unnecessary, or unusual noise which disturbs the peace or quiet of any neighborhood or which causes discomfort or annoyance to any reasonable person of normal sensitiveness residing in the area, which would include business areas, as well as public streets and sidewalks.
(4) It shall be unlawful for any person to willfully make or to continue or cause to be made or continued any loud, unnecessary or unusual noise by or within a motor vehicle which disturbs the peace or quiet of any neighborhood or which causes discomfort or annoyance to any reasonable person of normal sensitivity in the area, which would include business areas, as well as public streets and sidewalks. Included within this restriction are loud or no mufflers on motor vehicles and sound reproduction or amplification equipment in or on motor vehicles, such as radios, tape, cassette, CD or MP3 players, or megaphones, as well as any other device that transmits sound.
(5) It shall be unlawful for any person to willfully make or continue or cause to be made or continued between the hours of 8:00 p.m. and 6:00 a.m. that disturbs the peace or quiet of any neighborhood or which causes discomfort or annoyance to any reasonable person of normal sensitiveness in the area. The noise restriction of this subsection includes, but is not limited to, industrial or commercial operations situated in or near residential areas, garbage and other solid waste collection, and deliveries which generate the level of prohibited noise.
(b) The standards which may be considered in determining whether a violation of the provisions of this division exist may include, but not be limited to, the following:
(1) The level of the noise;
(2) The nature of the noise and whether it is usual or unusual;
(3) Whether the origin of the noise is natural or unnatural;
(4) The level and intensity of the background noise, if any;
(5) The proximity of the noise to residential sleeping facilities, hospitals, churches, and facilities for the care of the elderly, ill, or mentally ill, as well as any other area which historically is treated by the community as a quiet area;
(6) The density of the inhabitation of the area within which the noise emanates;
(7) The natures and zoning of the area within which the noise emanates;
(8) The time of day or night the noise occurs;
(9) Whether the noise is recurrent, intermittent, or constant.
(c) Vehicle stereo noise.
(1) in addition to the other restrictions and standards contained in this division, it shall be unlawful for any person operating or occupying a motor vehicle on a public street or other public property or other property used by the public to operate or amplify with sound produced by a radio, tape player, or other mechanical sound making device or instrument from within the motor vehicle so that the sound is plainly audible at a distance of 100 feet or more from said motor vehicle.
(2) The term "plainly audible" is defined as any loud, unnecessary or unusual noise which disturbs the peace or quiet of any neighborhood or which causes discomfort or annoyance to any reasonable person of normal sensitiveness residing in the rea which includes the business area as well as public streets and sidewalks in the county. This prohibition is in addition to and not exclusive of the other provisions of this division.
(3) The provisions of this section shall not apply to any law enforcement or emergency vehicle in the performance of lawful duties or to the noise made by a horn or other warning devise as required by O.C.G.A. § 40-8-70 or to any motor vehicle used for business or political purposes which in the normal course of such business use sound-making devices.
(Ord. of 3-6-2018)

731:
(a) Conditions constituting nuisance. Any building, structure, enclosure, dwelling or premises is a nuisance if it meets one or more of the following criteria:
(1) Those buildings or dwellings which are hazardous to life or property by reason of the condition or quantity of the building or dwelling contents, use of the building or dwelling or its contents, the enclosure or the overcrowding of the building at any time of persons therein;
(2) Those with deficiencies in fire alarm or fire prevention equipment;
(3) Those which have conditions of construction of the building or dwelling likely to cause fire, the spreading of fire, or of conditions therein which would hamper or impede the fire department of the county in combating a fire in or about the building or dwelling;
(4) Those which have become so dilapidated, decayed, unsafe, unsanitary or otherwise incapable of providing the amenities essential to decent living so as to be likely to cause sickness or disease, so as to work injury to the health, morals, safety or to otherwise be detrimental to the general welfare of those living therein;
(5) Those whose interior walls or other vertical structural members list, lean or buckle to such an extent that a plumb line passing through the center of gravity falls outside of the middle third of its base;
(6) Those which, exclusive of the foundation, show 33 percent or more of damage or deterioration of the supporting member or members, or 50 percent of damage or deterioration of the nonsporting enclosing or outside walls or covering;
(7) Those which have improperly distributed loads upon the floors or roofs or are overloaded, or which have insufficient strength to be reasonably safe for the purpose used;
(8) Those which have been damaged by fire, wind or other causes to as to have become dangerous to life, safety, morals or the general health and welfare of the occupants or the people of the county;
(9) Those having light, air, or sanitation facilities which are inadequate to protect the health, morals, safety or general welfare of human beings who live or may live thereon;
(10) Those having inadequate means of egress in case of fire or panic or those having insufficient stairways, elevators, fire escapes or other means of ingress or egress;
(11) Those which have parts thereof which are so impaired that they may fall and injure members of the public or property;
(12) Those which, because of their condition, are generally unsafe, unsanitary or dangerous to the health, morals, safety or general welfare of the people of the county;
(13) Any act, condition, use or other occurrence which violates several (five or more) provisions of the standard codes as listed in section 10-21.
(b) The owner or occupant in possession of any abandoned, vacant or fire-damaged buildings or structures shall secure and board up such buildings or structures to prevent entry by animals, vermin or trespassers. The structure shall be deemed secure if the windows, doors and other openings are boarded up, or in the case of sold doors, the doors are securely locked.
(c) It shall be the duty of the inspector, fire chief or his designee to insure that structures rendered uninhabitable due to fire damage shall be boarded up and secured within 24 hours.
(d) The inspector, the fire chief or his designee, after determining that any building, structure, dwelling or condition may constitute a nuisance, may nonetheless deviate from the strict provisions of abatement contain in Division III of this article, based upon the inspector's finding that one of the following criteria exists which creates a "hardship" which warrants a decision not to abate a nuisance, to wit:
(1) That the type of structure, topography of the land, or other unusual circumstance indicates that the normal time period for abatement of any such nuisance should be extended; or
(2) That due to the health, mental or physical infirmity, or similar serious medical problem of the owner, or serious and dire financial circumstances of the owner and/or the requirements of abatement, the inspector determines to grant a moratorium, or an additional period of time, or to otherwise modify the requirements of abatement to suit the particular circumstances of the occurrence.
(e) Procedure for abatement. Whenever the inspector has information that a building, structure or dwelling may be in such condition that it is a nuisance, the inspector shall cause an examination thereof to be made. If, upon such examination, the inspector finds that any building, structure or dwelling meets any of the criteria in subsection (a) of this section, or that any other conditions exist which in the opinion of the inspector shall constitute a nuisance, it shall be the duty of the inspector to give notice pursuant to section 26-139 to the owner, occupants and all parties in interest of such building, structure or dwelling or to the person in possession, charge or control thereof. If the owner, occupant and parties in interest fail to abate the nuisance in accordance with the notice, the inspector shall then cause summons and complaint to be issued requiring the parties to appear before the judge of the magistrate court, as provided by section 26-139, and other provisions of this article The owner, occupant and parties in interest shall appear and show cause before the judge of the magistrate court as provided generally in O.C.G.A. § 41-2-5 and this Code to determine what action and decision, if any, the judge of the magistrate court shall take concerning any alleged nuisance.
(Ord. of 3-6-2018)
